{
  "heading": "Validation: Tac",
  "axis": "language",
  "summary": "The same seven markers work in Tac, on a service instead of a controller. The data transfer object (DTO) that `Check` names is a model: a data class in its own file, `client/models/OrderInput.<ext>` (ADR 0092). Discovery composes its rules into the service that it protects (ADR 0044), and the model itself compiles once, without its markers. A service reaches it the way the language reaches any model: C# and F# open `Models`, Rust uses `crate::models::OrderInput`, Kotlin imports `models.OrderInput` and Dart imports `models/OrderInput.dart`; Swift, Go and Ruby need no import. A Swift model is a struct, and a Rust model and its fields are `pub`. JavaScript, TypeScript and Dart keep Service without an import, as their Yon services do. The compiled languages mark the service with Service too: the build refuses a service in client/services without its marker with TY1363. Swift and C# take checked parameters as BehaviorJSON, Rust and Go take them as BehaviorValue, and Java takes them as Object, as its Yon service does; Check names the class, as in Yon. Tac validation protects only the user experience (UX). It is not authorization, so Yon must validate the same payload again.",
  "body": "Lua has no tab here, because the build refuses Check in Lua Tac code with TY1308. Python has no tab either: Python Tac does not read a Checked model from client/models yet, so the build stops a Python Check with TY1308.",
